    "q-341280": {
      "dom_id": "step-q341280",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=7{x}^{2}+4$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=7{x}^{2}+4$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(7{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{3}+4x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{7{\\pi }^{3}}{3}+4\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(7{\\pi }^{2}+12).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
